=== STEP commit: git commit -m Scheduler: a worker that outlived its service, rounds that were skipped, and four smaller faults

CI for this branch stood until the 25-minute limit. An independent review
of why found that the same stop is on main and on another branch, before
any of the tests here, so the faults below are not what made CI red. They
are what could have given the same symptom, and two of them are on main
today.

- The worker loop went through `self?` for both the round and the sleep.
  A service that was let go of without stop() left a loop that never
  waited and kept a thread for the rest of the process. The loop now holds
  the service only while a round runs and sleeps through a copy of the
  closure, and deinit cancels the worker. (On main today.)
- runDueEvents() returned at once, having done nothing, when a round was
  already running. It now waits for that round and then makes its own,
  with its own look at the clock. (On main today.)
- start() on a service whose worker already runs took the worker's own
  run for an interrupted one and counted it twice. Only the call that
  takes the worker lock recovers interrupted runs.
- The lock on the state file was waited for without end, on a thread
  Swift concurrency shares. The wait is now five seconds, and the change
  then fails with "Cannot take the lock".
- BoundedProcessRunner: a poll interval of zero made its loop spin. The
  pause is never below a hundredth of a second.
- Tests. In ScheduledEventScheduleTests, a test that starts a worker and
  can fail before it reaches stop() now stops the worker on every way
  out. The two such tests in ScheduledEventServiceTests are main's and
  are left as they are; the first point makes them harmless. The process
  tests have time limits of 1.5 seconds instead of 0.3 and 0.4, the suite
  that changes signal handling for the whole process is serialized, and
  a child that is left behind on purpose is killed also when the test
  fails early.

Four new tests, one for each of the first four points. Each goes red when
its correction is taken out again (tried on Linux, Swift 6.2.3).

macOS, this tree, Swift 6.4: swift build --build-tests without warnings,
the scheduler suites three times and swift test --no-parallel twice: 398
tests in 59 suites, none failed. The compiled tests also pass through the
runner from 5dc229f on codex/file-provenance-20261002, in 28 of its 120
seconds. Linux: the 75 tests in the four scheduler suites, ten runs of ten.

